In article <2t7eu3$8j2@portal.gmu.edu>, tvalesky@mason1.gmu.edu (Thomas B Valesky) writes:
>I've spent more time playing Nethack than on any 10 other games
>combined. However, when I try to explain the wonders of Nethack to an
>unbeliever, I can't seem to put the reasons for my love of the game to
>them. Anybody care to try to write a poem or something about "Why
>Nethack is the greatest game in the history of the human race?"

Because it is very carefully written, with care for every detail and every
(even very improbable) combination of conditions. This makes it full of
surprises. Also you seldom have the feeling 'if it was real life I'd do ...,
but the game does not allow it'... This is the function of many years
of development, and thus very difficult to achieve in a new game, and
plainly impossible in any commercial games (which company will invest
5 years and use tens of thousands test players to develop one game?)

Because it is so difficult that solving it alone, without any spoilers
requires normally several years of play. Compare to your typical commercial
game, which you solve within a week, and loose interest after another one.
It is the mistery of the unknown that keeps you playing it.

Because there is no unique strategy for winning it. You can win just hacking,
or by playing 'stealthy' or building on magic and magic devices - through 
it is seldom that one 'fixed' strategy will lead to win.

Because even if you won, you may be sure that you still don't know all 
tricks and all items - so you are willing to start again, trying another
character and another strategy.

Enough reasons? No? You may also add that it simply makes fun :-)
